1 TYPE

   Writes a file to the terminal, a line for each record.

   Format

     TYPE filespec

2 Parameter

 filespec

   One file, on a mounted volume or on the host.  A wildcard must match
   one file only.  For a volume file, a version left out is the newest,
   and the other parts left out are the default device and directory's
   (see SET DEFAULT).

   A name with a host path ("./notes.txt", quoted to keep its case) is a
   host file, and so is a bare name when the default directory isn't on
   a volume.  A host file is shown line by line as it is.

   TYPE SYS$INPUT types the process's input: in a command procedure, the
   data lines after the command, up to the next "$" line (see @ and
   DECK); at the terminal, what is typed, up to Ctrl/Z.

2 Description

   A volume file's records are shown as text, whatever their format
   (fixed, variable, VFC, or stream), as COPY shows them when it copies
   a file to the host as text.  An indexed file's records are shown in
   the order of its primary key.

2 Qualifiers
/HOST

   Makes filespec a host file name, whatever it looks like.  It may
   follow the verb or the file: TYPE/HOST "notes.txt" and TYPE
   "notes.txt"/HOST are the same.

/PAGE

   Shows the file a screenful at a time.  After each screen, TYPE asks
   "Press RETURN to continue": Return shows the next screen, and any
   text typed before Return ends the display.  The screen is the
   terminal's size, or when that can't be read, the vax.console.lines
   setting's height (24 lines by default).

2 Examples

   1.$ TYPE FOO.TXT

     Shows the newest FOO.TXT in the default directory.

   2.$ TYPE/PAGE DUA1:[WORK]README.TXT;2

     Shows version 2 of README.TXT a screen at a time.

   3.$ TYPE "README.md"/HOST

     Shows the host file README.md.
